{"name":"Dijonnaise","hex":"#9b8f55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Dijonnaise","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sauce combining Dijon mustard and mayonnaise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Dijonnaise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dijon mustard","blocks":[{"text":"Dijon mustard is a traditional mustard from France. It is named after the city of Dijon in Burgundy, which was the center of mustard making in the late Middle Ages and was granted exclusive rights in France in the 17th century. First used in 1336 for the table of King Philip VI, it assumed its current form in 1856 when Jean Naigeon of Dijon replaced the vinegar usually used in prepared mustard with verjuice, the acidic juice of unripe grapes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg/330px-Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dijon_mustard"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg/500px-Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg/1280px-Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg/500px-Dijon_mustard_on_a_spoon_-_20051218.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASenf-5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Maille_Dijon_Originale.jpg/500px-Maille_Dijon_Originale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"htt